    {
      "question": "q-37570",
      "field": null,
      "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
      "old_sha256": "2844ce5c0bfe17500e662dc8d7cf95c3ec596454a79f8c882e251ff664330bf9",
      "new_sha256": "ef885154694bdbd48d7107de622c13f31d20517c8646e3ee26641a85c673aab6",
      "category": "ma_capture",
      "authoring": [],
      "source": [
        {
          "question": "q-37570",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,2)$ can be found by computing \n$\u2207f(1,-1,2).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u2207f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{\u00a0}j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{\u00a0}k \\\\  & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}(xy+5x+{z}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(xy+5x+{z}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202z}(xy+5x+{z}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =(y+5)\\text{\u00a0}i+x\\text{\u00a0}j+2z\\text{\u00a0}k \\\\ \u2207f(1,-1,2) & =(-1+5)\\,i+(1)\\,j+2(2)\\,k \\\\  & =4\\,i+j+4\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $4\\,i+j+4\\,k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,2).$",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067198/state.json",
          "file_sha256": "99704f3b48f88db4fd7ca174fb2eab96166a8f624c2c49908f1bc18899b8b71b"
        }
